    "body": "Problem:\r\nlibuv timers count from the loop's cached \"now\", updated once per loop\r\niteration. libuv caches it to avoid a clock read per timer start\r\n(historically a syscall on older kernels, etc.). Nvim runs Lua/Vimscript\r\nin loop callbacks, so after blocking code a timer fires at the next loop\r\nwakeup: thus e.g. `vim.system()` timeout kills the process immediately,\r\n`jobstop()` sends SIGKILL before the 2s grace period, etc.\r\n\r\nSolution:\r\nCall `uv_update_time()` before starting these timers.\r\n\r\n\r\nfix https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/42521\r\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-10-31T11:38:08Z",
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "_links": {
                "html": {
                    "href": "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/42615#discussion_r4431486460"
                },
                "pull_request": {
                    "href": "https://api.github.com/repos/neovim/neovim/pulls/42615"
                },
                "self": {
                    "href": "https://api.github.com/repos/neovim/neovim/pulls/comments/4431486460"
                }
            },
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "If we used `vim.fn.timer_start()` then we wouldn't need to manually do this at each caller. Yet more evidence that using raw `vim.uv` stuff is causing extra work, we should be using the abstractions we already have.\n\nIn particular lsp/buf.lua and tty.lua wrap their callbacks in `vim.schedule`, so they could use `timer_start`. Other cases like `vim.system` need raw `uv.new_timer` for \"fast\" contexts.",
            "commit_id": "661b6fd7f79e27f9e3ec7f08d52cb0ae13685f91",
            "copilot_code_review_sources": [],
            "created_at": "2026-10-31T11:03:29Z",
            "diff_hunk": "@@ -649,6 +649,7 @@ function vim.defer_fn(fn, timeout)\n   vim.validate('fn', fn, 'callable', true)\n \n   local timer = assert(vim.uv.new_timer())\n+  vim.uv.update_time() -- See time_watcher_start().\n   timer:start(timeout, 0, function()",
